---
/**
 * The twenty-six words, on the page that argues about them.
 *
 * Week 2 is titled "Alfa to Zulu" and spends six hundred words on acoustic
 * distance, on `NOVEMBER` surviving the loss of its first syllable, and on two
 * words being misspelled on purpose — and the site never showed the alphabet.
 * A lecture whose title promises A to Z should produce A to Z.
 *
 * ## Why a grid and not a table
 *
 * The glossary's tables are for looking a thing up: three or four columns, one
 * row per entry, read across. This is the opposite — twenty-six pairs of two
 * short strings, read as a *set*, because the lecture's claim is about the
 * words' relation to each other (how far apart BRAVO, CHARLIE, DELTA and ECHO
 * are pushed). A 26-row table buries that; an auto-fitting grid puts them in
 * sight of one another and reflows to a single column on a phone without a
 * breakpoint of its own.
 *
 * ## The marked pair
 *
 * ALFA and JULIETT carry the lecture's argument, so they are marked rather
 * than left for the reader to spot. The mark is the same dotted-underline
 * idiom the glossary uses for a form to notice, because in both places it
 * means the same thing: look at this one, it is not what you expect.
 *
 * The *reason* is deliberately not in the cell. The first draft printed it
 * there, and rendering showed two costs: the note made its own row half again
 * as tall as the other four, so a 26-cell grid read as lopsided with five
 * empty tall cells beside ALFA — and worse, it said the same thing as the
 * section immediately below, which is titled "The two misspellings" and
 * explains both at length. The mark points; the prose explains. Duplicating
 * the prose inside the figure is how a page starts arguing with itself.
 */
import { respelledDigits, spellingAlphabet } from "../data/phraseology";
---

<figure class="alphabet">
  <ol class="grid" aria-label="The ICAO spelling alphabet, A to Z">
    {
      spellingAlphabet.map((entry) => (
        <li class={entry.deliberate ? "cell marked" : "cell"}>
          <span class="letter">{entry.letter}</span>
          <span class="word">{entry.word}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p class="digits">
    <span>And the three digits respelled outright: </span>
    {
      respelledDigits.map((d, i) => (
        <span>
          {i > 0 ? ", " : ""}
          <strong>{d.digit}</strong> as <code>{d.spoken}</code>
        </span>
      ))
    }
    <span>.</span>
  </p>

  <figcaption>
    Two of the twenty-six are marked, because they are misspelled in English on
    purpose. Both misspellings are about somebody else's mouth rather than your
    own — which is the next section.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .alphabet {
    margin-block: 2rem;
    margin-inline: 0;
  }

  .grid {
    display: grid;
    /* Reflows from a single column on a phone up to six or seven at desktop
       without naming a breakpoint. 7rem fits the longest word, NOVEMBER, at
       the body size. */
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(7rem, 1fr));
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .cell {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.4rem 1fr;
    gap: 0 0.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.55rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .letter {
    color: var(--at-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .word {
    font-family: var(--at-font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }

  /* Same idiom as the glossary's deviation forms: in both places the mark
     means "look at this one, it is not what you expect". */
  .marked {
    border-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--at-secondary) 45%, transparent);
  }

  .marked .word {
    color: var(--at-secondary);
    text-decoration: underline dotted;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .digits {
    margin-block: 1rem 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  figcaption {
    color: var(--at-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>
